<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" ?>

<section xml:id="sec-gas6" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
	<title>Ideal Gas Law and Chemical Reactions</title>

	<p>
		The Ideal Gas Law can also be used in calculations involving chemical
		reactions.
		The Ideal Gas Law can be used to determine the number of moles of reactants or
		to determine the pressure/volume/temperature of the products from the number
		of moles.
		This is displayed in the figure below.
	</p>

	<figure>
		<caption>Flow Chart for Stoichiometry Problems Involving Gases</caption>
		<image source="stoichiometry_conversion_flowchart.svg">
			<shortdescription>
				A flow chart that shows multiple ways to solve stoichiometry problems by
				interconverting between moles and gas parameters or by converting from moles
				to grams
			</shortdescription>
		</image>
	</figure>

	<p>
		We can see various ways that this figure can be utilized in problem solving as
		shown in the sample problems below:
	</p>

	<assemblage>
		<title>Sample calculations</title>

		<p>
			A vessel with a volume of 1200 L contains 17 kg of nitrogen gas and exactly
			the amount of hydrogen gas needed to react with it at a temperature of
			<m>773 K</m>.
			The nitrogen and hydrogen gas react according to the equation shown below:
		</p>

		<p>
			<me>
				N_2\ (g)\ +\ 3H_2\ (g)\ \rightarrow\ 2NH_3\ (g)\
			</me>
		</p>

		<p>
			<ol>
				<li>
					<p>
						How much hydrogen (in moles) is needed to react with the nitrogen gas?
					</p>
				</li>

				<li>
					<p>
						What is the starting pressure of the gases in the tank in units of psi?
					</p>
				</li>

				<li>
					<p>
						What is the pressure in the tank at the conclusion of the reaction in
						units of psi?
					</p>
				</li>
			</ol>
		</p>

		<p>
			<term>Solutions:</term>
		</p>

		<p>
			<em>To start all of the solutions:</em> We are given the initial mass of the
			nitrogen gas in kg.
			The first step to solving any reaction stoichiometry problem is to first
			figure out the number of moles of the substance that you have information
			about.
			So in this case, we have to convert from kg of nitrogen gas to moles of
			nitrogen gas using the molar mass
		</p>

		<p>
			<me>
				17kg\ N_2\cdot\frac{1000g}{1kg}\cdot\frac{mole}{28.02g}=606.7 moles\ N_2
			</me>
		</p>

		<p>
			We will use this number as the starting point for each of the other
			calculations
		</p>

		<p>
			<em>Solution to Problem 1:</em> We need to convert from moles of nitrogen gas
			to moles of hydrogen gas.
			To convert from moles of one substance to moles of another in a chemical
			reaction, we need a mole-mole conversion factor:
		</p>

		<p>
			<me>
				\frac{3\ moles\ H_2}{1\ mole\ N_2}
			</me>
		</p>

		<p>
			We can then multiply our number of moles of nitrogen gas by this conversion
			factor as shown:
		</p>

		<p>
			<me>
				606.7\ moles\ N_2\cdot\frac{3\ moles\ H_2}{1\ mole\ N_2}=1800\ moles\ H_2
			</me>
		</p>

		<p>
			<em>Solution to Problem 2:</em> We are going to use the Ideal Gas Law and
			plug in the total number of moles of nitrogen gas and hydrogen gas.
			We should add the number of moles together and then rearrange the Ideal Gas
			Law to solve for pressure
		</p>

		<p>
			Total number of moles of gas:
		</p>

		<p>
			<me>
				606.7\ moles\ N_2\ + 1820\ moles\ H_2=2427\ moles\ gas
			</me>
		</p>

		<p>
			Rearranging the Ideal Gas Law:
		</p>

		<p>
			<me>
				PV=nRT\ \rightarrow\ \frac{PV}{V}=\frac{nRT}{V}/ /rightarrow\ P=\frac{nRT}{V}
			</me>
		</p>

		<p>
			We can now plug in the values from the problem to solve for P:
		</p>

		<p>
			<me>
				P=\frac{2427\ moles\cdot\ 1.21\frac{L\cdot psi}{mol\cdot K}\cdot\ 773K}{1200\ L}= 1886 psi\rightarrow\ 1900\ psi
			</me>
		</p>

		<p>
			<em>Solution to Problem 3:</em>
		</p>

		<p>
			We can start with the moles of nitrogen gas that we've already calculated.
			If we have moles of nitrogen gas, we can use a mole-mole conversion factor to
			get the number of moles of <m>NH_3</m>.
			_ We can then use the Ideal Gas Law to calculate the pressure.
		</p>

		<p>
			<me>
				606.7\ moles\ N_2\cdot\frac{2\ moles\ NH_3}{1\ mole\ N_2} = 1213.4\ moles\ NH_3
			</me>
		</p>

		<p>
			<me>
				PV=nRT\rightarrow P=\frac{nRT}{V}\rightarrow P=\frac{1213.4\ moles\cdot\ 1.21\frac{L\cdot psi}{moles\cdot K}\cdot\ 773K}{1200\ L}=943\ psi\rightarrow\ 940 psi
			</me>
		</p>
	</assemblage>

	<p/>
	<exercises>
		<title>Exercises</title>

		<exercise>
			<statement>
				<p>
					When our bodies 'burn' glucose ( <m>C_6H_{12}O_6</m> ), they do so
					according to the following reaction:
				</p>

				<p>
					<me>
						C_6H_{12}O_6\ (g)\ +\ 6O_2\ (g)\ \rightarrow\ 6CO_2\ (g)\ +\ 6H_2O/ (l)
					</me>
				</p>

				<p>
					If a person consumes a soda with 42 g of glucose, what volume of carbon
					dioxide will they emit at STP once they've burned all that glucose?
				</p>
			</statement>

			<answer>
				<p>
					31 L
				</p>
			</answer>
		</exercise>

		<exercise>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Water can undergo an electrolysis reaction shown below:
				</p>

				<p>
					<me>
						2H_2O\ (l)\ \rightarrow\ O_2\ (g)\
					 		+\ 2H_2\ (g)
					</me>
				</p>

				<p>
					If 33 g of water undergoes this reaction in a sealed chamber with a volume
					of 2.0 L at a temperature of 301K, then what will the pressure be in the
					chamber at the end of the reaction in MPa?
				</p>
			</statement>

			<answer>
				<p>
					3.4MPa
				</p>
			</answer>
		</exercise>
	</exercises>
</section>